libbtbb (2026-10-R1) unstable; urgency=low

  [ Decoding (BR/EDR) ]
  * AUX1, EV3, EV4 and EV5 payloads decode, each at the longest length its
    type allows.
  * A DV packet's data field decodes, with its own whitening rather than the
    voice field's.
  * An HV3 voice packet is reported as HV3, not as 32 bytes of EV3.
  * An eSCO payload's length comes from btbb_packet_set_esco_length(), read
    back by btbb_packet_get_esco_length(), or from the longest length whose
    CRC agrees rather than the first.
  * A packet whose payload did not decode, or is shorter than its own CRC,
    reports no payload and is not called CRC-checked.
  * A packet cut short decodes no payload and no header out of symbols the
    capture did not hold.
  * New flags BTBB_HEC_CHECKED, BTBB_HEC_VALID and BTBB_CRC_CHECKED join
    BTBB_CRC_CORRECT to say what the decoder checked and what it found.
  * New btbb_find_ac_n() takes the symbol stream's length and reads no
    further; btbb_find_ac(), which carries no length, is unchanged.

  [ Decoding (LE) ]
  * An extended advertising PDU keeps its whole payload: the Length is read
    from the whole header octet, as the Core specification defines it.
  * The LE symbol buffer is 264 bytes, so a Length of 252 to 255 keeps its
    last octets and its real CRC.
  * An LE data PDU may carry the 251 bytes Data Length Extension permits, and
    capture files record all of it rather than its first 64 bytes.
  * New lell_allocate_and_decode_n() takes the bytes captured, so it cannot
    read past a caller's buffer; lell_get_payload_length() reads the length.
  * A malformed advertising payload is counted, not reported line by line, by
    lell_malformed_count(); lell_set_malformed_verbose() asks for the detail.
  * A service UUID list whose length is not a whole number of UUIDs prints the
    whole ones it holds, and a short 16-bit list is counted.
  * A Tx Power Level prints as the signed value the specification defines, so
    lell_print() reports -12 dBm rather than 244 dBm on every host.
  * An advertising PDU shorter than the fields read from it says what is
    missing, and a data PDU with no payload reports no LL Control opcode.
  * A flags element with no flags in it no longer has a flags byte read from
    past the end of the advertising data.
  * A frequency outside 2402 to 2480 MHz gives a bounded channel index.
  * Manufacturer-specific data names 4133 companies from the Bluetooth SIG's
    published Assigned Numbers, where BlueZ's gave 1038, and keeps the 73.
  * LE decode complaints go to standard error rather than into the packet dump
    on standard output, unchanged in wording.

  [ Clock and hopping ]
  * A clock search on a piconet using adaptive frequency hopping recognizes
    the adapted hopping and keeps narrowing toward a clock, where it used to
    start over on the basic sequence and never find one.
  * Adaptive frequency hopping (AFH) follows the Core specification (Vol 2,
    Part B, 2.6.3.1), so a clock tracked with AFH names the right channels.
  * An adaptive piconet is not handed the hopping sequence cached for the same
    address and a different channel map.
  * A clock and UAP that are not the piconet's are given up: 20 failed
    headers, or 10 after two seconds, give BTBB_PROCESS_LOCK_LOST.
  * New btbb_piconet_drop_clock() hands back a clock whose only symptom is
    silence; a clock given up is not chosen again, and a caller's is kept.
  * Clock recovery needs four further hops to agree before it accepts a
    candidate clock, which accepts a wrong one about a tenth as often.
  * A piconet that nothing works for is reported once, after three clocks are
    given up, rather than on every loss.
  * btbb_piconet_get_clk_offset() reports the offset once CLK1-6 is known, in
    625 us slots; btbb.h says which clock each stage of recovery reports.
  * New btbb_survey_piconet(lap) hands back the live piconet for a LAP and
    leaves it in the survey, so a tool can read a recovered UAP.
  * btbb_init_hop_reversal() refuses aliased hop reversal, where 79 channels
    are observed folded into 25, with a note on standard error.
  * Reading a piconet's AFH map answers hops one at a time, without the 128 MB
    allocation and the wait a whole hopping sequence cost.
  * The hopping sequence is gone, with its notes on standard output and the
    exported hop(), gen_hop_pattern() and get_hop_pattern().

  [ Capture files ]
  * The record of the packet that settles a piconet's address part holds that
    packet's own header and payload together, with the HEC flags.
  * A record carries only a payload the decoder produced for that packet, not
    a trial clock's result from guessing the upper address part.
  * BR/EDR records say whether the header's HEC and the payload's CRC were
    checked and whether they held, in both PCAP and PCAPNG files.
  * BR/EDR records carry the number of bits forward error correction put
    right, where both fields read 0 before whatever the reception.
  * The connection parameters a PCAPNG file records for a CONNECT_IND are the
    ones the packet carried, so a reader can follow the connection.
  * lell_pcapng_append_packet() returns the positive
    LELL_PCAPNG_CONNECT_INFO_NOT_RECORDED when a long LE PCAPNG capture runs
    out of room for a CONNECT_IND's parameters, so a tool can warn once.
  * An LE capture file's reference access address flag follows the address the
    caller gave rather than the channel, as the published layout defines.
  * The snapshot length a capture file declares is the longest record its
    writer can produce: 422 bytes for BR/EDR, 274 for LE and 288 for PPI.
  * A failed or incomplete write is reported, and the part that reached the
    file taken back out, so a full disk ends the capture on a whole record.
  * btbb_pcapng_close() and lell_pcapng_close() return 0 on success.
  * The interface options a PCAPNG file records for a CONNECT_IND, a BD_ADDR
    and a clock carry the length of their contents.
  * A pcapng capture file is created in a few milliseconds rather than a few
    hundred; the bytes written are unchanged.
  * The captured and original lengths in LE PCAP records are the right way
    round, and the PCAPNG writer copies no more than the record holds.

  [ Output ]
  * Decoding a packet does not print it; btbb_print_packet() prints a packet
    as it always has.
  * Progress and complaints from UAP and clock discovery go to standard error;
    the AFH map btbb_print_afh_map() prints stays on standard output.
  * An allocation the library cannot get is reported: the FEC 2/3 decoder,
    tun_format() and btbb_piconet_new() return NULL, btbb_init() returns -1.
  * A failed allocation in one of the library's hash tables prints "error:
    libbtbb: out of memory" and exits 1, where it exited 255 in silence.

  [ Build and install ]
  * Builds and installs out of the box on current tools: the CMake minimum is
    3.5, and the Python tools install from a PEP 440 wheel.
  * The library and the header go where the system keeps them, from
    GNUInstallDirs; CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR, CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR and
    LIB_SUFFIX choose otherwise.
  * libbtbb.pc names the directories the files are really in and works out its
    prefix from its own location, so a staged or moved install still links.
  * The cleanup script looks in lib64 and a multiarch directory as well as
    lib, and finds the pkg-config file, a static archive, a .dylib and btbb.h.
  * The library exports only the functions btbb.h declares, 96 names where 128
    were exported; one of them, bt_compidtostr(), clashed with BlueZ's.
  * On Windows the BTBB_API macro each declaration in btbb.h carries is the
    dllexport or dllimport declaration a shared library needs.
  * The soname stays libbtbb.so.1 and the library version is 1.3, where
    2020-12-R1's was 1.0; abidiff finds nine functions gained, none lost.
  * Existing callers see three changes behind that interface: two functions
    answer a positive value where they answered 0, the writers report a write
    that missed the file, and a decode says less where it knows less.
  * A build asking for no library, "cmake -DBUILD_SHARED_LIB=OFF", is refused
    while configuring, naming both options it takes.
  * The RPM spec, the MacPorts Portfile and the Homebrew formula in packaging/
    are gone; each of those projects keeps its own packaging.

  [ Python tools ]
  * btaptap decodes keystrokes again; the recorded Ellisys export, 864
    keyboard reports, gives up the paragraph that was typed into it.
  * btaptap reports a capture it cannot read through as one "error:" line and
    a non-zero exit, where it exited 0 or raised a traceback.
  * btaptap's command line is checked: an unknown option, a missing value, a
    repeated value, -r with -e, and -c without -r each exit 1.
  * btaptap's -h prints the usage text on standard output and no longer
    cancels a capture given beside it.
  * The pcapdump module writes a capture file again, and takes a timestamp's
    unit from the capture's magic number, so nanosecond files read as such.
  * The pcapdump module refuses a file that is not a capture, reads one taken
    at a snapshot length, and writes a record's two lengths in order.
  * btaptap finds the pcapdump module of the install it belongs to, and
    installing into a second prefix puts the module there too.
  * The scripts in python/utils that produced the committed tables run on
    python 3, and gen_barker_correct.py reproduces the barker tables.

  [ Under the hood ]
  * Clock recovery no longer writes about 200 kB past its candidate list under
    adaptive hopping.
  * The first packet of a recovery attempt no longer adapts to an AFH map the
    library never observed.
  * Hop predictions follow the AFH map as it is learned, one hop at a time
    rather than from a 128 MB table.
  * Choosing the CLK1-27 candidates walks the 2^21 clock values once, and
    btbb_winnow() stops once the clock is known.
  * The hop pattern remembered while reversing hops is bounded, and a piconet
    released during clock recovery no longer leaks its candidate list.
  * btbb_next_survey_result() takes the piconet's entry out of the survey's
    hash table as well as its list, and the table is freed.
  * The 625 us slot offset and the 312.5 us CLKN tick offset are separate
    fields, and the UAP is widened before it is shifted into an address.
  * An allocation failure in the LE decoder is no longer dereferenced.
  * An interface description of 253 characters or more is no longer read past
    the end of the option list that carries it.
  * A PCAPNG header block's spare room is filled with as many padding options
    as it takes, and options past that room are refused rather than written.
  * A PCAPNG record no longer carries three bytes of the caller's memory in
    the padding that rounds it to a 4-byte boundary, in either writer.
  * A no-payload record, a high reference UAP, the LE decoder, the dump, and
    an unaligned read of 16-bit UUIDs no longer rely on undefined behavior.

